{{!
    @template local_byblos/docs/sharing

    Guide: sharing pages and collections (user / course / group / public).

    Context variables:
    - links (object) Map of topic key => URL.
    - dashurl (string) Portfolio dashboard URL.
    - newpageurl (string) New-page wizard URL.

    @package    local_byblos
    @copyright  2026 South African Theological Seminary
    @license    http://www.gnu.org/copyleft/gpl.html GNU GPL v3 or later

    Example context (json):
    {
        "dashurl": "#",
        "newpageurl": "#",
        "pix": {"manage_sharing": "#"},
        "links": {
            "index": "#", "getting_started": "#", "building_pages": "#",
            "publishing": "#", "collections": "#", "sharing": "#",
            "assessment": "#", "pedagogy": "#", "best_practices": "#",
            "usecase_programme": "#", "usecase_course": "#",
            "usecase_professional": "#"
        }
    }
}}
<div class="byblos-docs-article">

    <h1>{{#str}}docs_topic_sharing, local_byblos{{/str}}</h1>
    <p class="byblos-docs-lead">
        Sharing decides <em>who</em> can see your work. You can share a single page or a whole
        collection with one person, a whole course, a group, or, where your site allows it, anyone holding a private link. Remember to <a href="{{{links.publishing}}}">publish</a> too;
        sharing a draft shows the viewer nothing.
    </p>

    <h2>Opening the share screen</h2>
    <ol class="byblos-docs-steps">
        <li>Open the page, and click <strong>{{#str}}sharethispage, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong>; or open the collection and click <strong>{{#str}}sharethiscollection, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong>.</li>
        <li>The sharing screen lists who already has access and lets you add more.</li>
    </ol>

    <figure class="byblos-docs-figure">
        <img src="{{pix.manage_sharing}}" class="byblos-docs-img" loading="lazy"
             alt="The Manage sharing screen with the share-type selector, the scoped picker, and a table of existing shares.">

        <figcaption>Figure 1: The sharing screen with its scoped pickers.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Choosing who to share with</h2>
    <p>Pick a share type, then choose from the list, Byblos only offers people and places you are actually connected to, so there are no IDs to look up:</p>
    <ul class="byblos-docs-deflist">
        <li><strong>{{#str}}share_user, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong>: one named person from the courses you share. Use this to share with an individual teacher or assessor.</li>
        <li><strong>{{#str}}share_course, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong>: everyone enrolled in a course you choose. Use this to share with a whole class or cohort.</li>
        <li><strong>{{#str}}share_group, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong>: everyone in one of your groups.</li>
        <li><strong>{{#str}}share_public, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong>: generates a secret link. <em>{{#str}}share_public_hint, local_byblos{{/str}}</em> Use this for people outside Moodle, such as an employer. (Available only if your site enables public sharing.)</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="byblos-docs-tip">
        <strong>Sharing a teacher into your work.</strong> To hand a single teacher access, choose
        <strong>{{#str}}share_user, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong> and pick their name. To let an entire
        class see it (for example a peer showcase), choose
        <strong>{{#str}}share_course, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong>.
    </div>

    <h2>Collections share as one unit</h2>
    <p>
        Share a collection and the viewer can open every page inside it through the collection's
        navigation, you do not have to share each page separately. This is the tidiest way to give an
        assessor a multi-page portfolio.
    </p>

    <h2>Revoking access</h2>
    <ol class="byblos-docs-steps">
        <li>Open the share screen for the page or collection.</li>
        <li>In the list of existing shares, click <strong>{{#str}}share_revoke, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong> on the entry you want to remove.</li>
        <li>Confirm. That person, course, group, or link immediately loses access.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Public links explained</h2>
    <p>
        A public share creates a long, unguessable URL that opens a clean, read-only view of your page
        or collection, no Moodle login required. Anyone you give the link to can view it, so treat it
        like a password: share it deliberately, and revoke it when it is no longer needed.
    </p>

    <div class="byblos-docs-note">
        <strong>What viewers see.</strong> Shared viewers see the published content only, never your
        editing controls, and never your drafts. You can check this yourself with "preview as viewer".
    </div>

    <h2>Inviting feedback on a page</h2>
    <p>
        Portfolios improve through dialogue, so you can let the people you share a page with leave
        feedback directly on it. At the bottom of one of your own pages, use the
        <strong>{{#str}}feedback_heading, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong> control to choose who may comment:
    </p>
    <ul class="byblos-docs-deflist">
        <li><strong>{{#str}}feedback_mode_off, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong>: no feedback (the default).</li>
        <li><strong>{{#str}}feedback_mode_teachers, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong>: only teachers (staff who can view shared work) may comment.</li>
        <li><strong>{{#str}}feedback_mode_cohort, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong>: everyone in the course or group you shared the page with may comment, so a whole learning cohort can respond.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Feedback is always attributed (there is no anonymous or public feedback), and a teacher's comment
        carries a teacher badge. As the page owner you moderate: you can <strong>hide</strong> or
        <strong>delete</strong> any comment, and anyone can delete their own.
    </p>

    <div class="byblos-docs-tip">
        <strong>Use it to revise.</strong> Frequent, constructive feedback is one of the conditions that
        makes an ePortfolio a high-impact practice (see <a href="{{{links.best_practices}}}">Best
        practices</a>). Turn feedback on while a page is a work in progress, gather responses, then act on
        them, that loop is where a lot of the learning happens.
    </div>

    <h2>Next</h2>
    <p>
        If your work is being graded, you may submit it through an assignment instead of (or as well as)
        sharing, see <a href="{{{links.assessment}}}">Submitting for assessment</a>.
    </p>
</div>
